Functionalization of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with nanoparticles (NPs) or biomolecules has attracted great attention in catalysis, separation, and biomedical applications.[1] Fabrication of nanoparticle-functionalized MOFs (NP/MOFs) was usually divided into three different strategies: one-pot method to induce the NPs within the cavities of MOF,[2] post-absorption of
NPs onto the MOF surfaces[3] and in situ growth of MOF on pre-synthesized NPs.[4] The NP/MOFs demonstrated efficient charge separation and excellent catalytic activity due to the uniform distribution of NPs and sensitization of MOFs.[5] On the other hand, the decoration of biomolecules such as enzyme, DNA and peptide into MOFs was bare due to the lack of mild synthetic conditions.[6] Initially, enzymes such as horseradish peroxidase were assembled on MOFs by surface attachment.[7] Later, a in situ encapsulated strategy was evolved to embed catalase into zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) matrix via a water-based mild approach.[8] In our previous work, a peptide-AuNP/ZIF8 core-shell nanocomposite with high activity was established in a mild condition for imaging intracellular enzymes.[9] To date, the simultaneous distribution o nanoparticles and enzymes in a MOF structure remains a great challenge, which inspires us to generate a programmable platform as tandem catalyst for biomedical applications.
Black phosphorus (BP) is a promising metal-free nanomaterial in biological fields due to its instinct structure and good biocompatibility.[10] In particular, BP quantum dot (BQ) displays amazing optical properties such as both singlet oxygen $({}^{1}\mathsf{O}_{2})$ production and photothermal conversion efficiency for photodynamic therapy (PDT) and photothermal therapy (PTT),[11] respectively, owing to the quantum confinement and edge effects.[12] Herein, for enhanced therapy in hypoxia,[13] a BQ and catalase co-encapsulated Materials of Institute Lavoisier (MIL)- type MOF heterostructure (BQ-MIL $@$ cat-MIL) was constructed by the precise assembly of BQ in MIL to form the BQ-MIL inner, and followed by introduction of catalase into the outer layer of homologous MIL (Scheme 1). Remarkably, the integrated MOF system could be utilized as a tandem catalyst to catalyze excess intracellular $H_{2}O_{2}$ to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ in MIL-protected catalase outer,[14] and then the latter was directly injected to and photocatalyzed by the MIL-sensitized BQ inner, leading to high quantum yield of singlet oxygen. Upon endocytosis, the nanoprobe demonstrated an 8.7- fold increase in photodynamic therapy efficiency than that without catalase, since the catalase protected by acid-resistant MOF could provide sufficient $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ for BQ-based PDT. Moreover, by coupling with photothermal therapy of BQs, photodynamicthermal synergistic therapy was achieved in vitro and in vivo. The homologous MOF nanocomposites integrated diverse functions such as sensitization and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ supply in a single block for more effective treatment against hypoxic conditions.
