$101\rightsquigarrow{1}\vdash{1}$ through stepwise in situ growth method. The BQs ranging from 2 to $10\ \mathsf{n m}$ (inset of Figure 1a) were uniformly distributed into the MIL inner to form the intermediate (BQ-MIL) with a diameter of ${\sim}60\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 1b). After the catalase enzyme was introduced in the MIL outer with a loading efficiency of $12\%$ , the resulting double-layered MOF (BQ-MIL@cat-MIL) demonstrated an octahedral spindle nanostructure with a size of $\sim140\ n m$ as shown in the transmission electron microscopic (TEM) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure 1a and 1c), which was consistent with the average hydrodynamic diameter of $145\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S1a). Meanwhile, BQMIL@cat-MIL showed a rough surface compared to the smooth surface of bare MIL@MIL (Figure 1d and S2), indicating the successful introduction of BQ and catalase into homologous MILs. Moreover, BQ-MIL@cat-MIL and BQ-MIL@MIL displayed an X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) pattern identical to that of pure MIL@MIL (Figure 1e), indicating the preservation of the crystalline structure even in the presence of BQ and catalase. From energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy elemental mapping images of BQ-MIL@cat-MIL, the P element $(15.8~\mathrm{wt\%})$ was mainly distributed in the core BQ-MIL that formed in the first growth, while the elements C, N, O, and Fe were homogenously distributed throughout the whole nanostructure (Figure S3), showing typical sectional concentric layers of BQ-MIL $@$ catMIL.[15] In addition, compared with pure MIL $@$ MIL, BQ-MIL@catMIL displayed slightly decreased Brunauer Emmett Teller surface areas due to the encapsulation of BQ and catalase (Figure 1f).
Furthermore, the amino groups on the BQ-MIL@cat-MIL surface were conveniently modified with HOOC-poly(ethylene glycol)-folic acid (PEG-FA) as the targeted element and cyanine 3 (Cy3)-labelled caspase substrate peptide (Cy3-pep) as the signal for monitoring cell apoptosis to obtain BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL nanoprobe, which remained stable in $10\%$ fetal bovine serum over $48\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure S1b and S4). Using methylene blue (MB) as the standard, $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ quantum yields of BQ, BQ-MIL@MIL, and BQMIL $@$ cat-fMIL were calculated to be $56.2\%$ , $90.1\%$ and $88.3\%$ by measuring the change of optical density $(\Delta\mathsf{O D})$ of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ indicator (1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran, DPBF) (Figure 2a and S5).[16] The enhanced $^10_{2}$ quantum yield was attributed to the photosensitivity of MIL skeleton. Different from the constant fluorescence emission wavelength between BQ and BQ
MIL@cat-fMIL, the phosphorescent emission wavelength of BQMIL@cat-fMIL revealed a blue-shift with a widened difference of $T_{1}.5_{0}$ (Figure 2b and Scheme 1), suggesting that more energy of excited BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL could be transferred to $^3{\sf O}_{2}$ to generate $^10_{2}$ and enhance the intrinsic $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ quantum yield. Next, the extra oxygen supply in different media was studied to further monitor the $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation (Figure 2c). In the presence of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ , ΔOD $(405~\mathsf{n m})$ of DPBF under hypoxia and nitrogen in BQMIL $@$ cat-fMIL were 4.5-fold and 12.8-fold higher than that in BQ-MIL $@M l$ without catalase, respectively. The efficient generation of $^10_{2}$ of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL was due to the combination between the sensitization of BQ-MIL inner and oxygen supply catalyzed by the encapsulated catalase which remained high activity in a hypoxic environment (Figure S6 and S7).
In addition, the temperature of BQ-MIL@cat-fMIL and BQ rose rapidly with the rise of 808 nm irradiation time and increased by $18.1~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $24.0~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ after $5\mathrm{\min}$ laser irradiation (Figure 2d), since BQ exhibited a high photothermal conversion efficiency of $23.3\%$ and excellent photothermal stability after functionalization (Figure S8). By contrast, the MIL $@M l$ and water only showed a minimal temperature change of $4.0\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $2.2\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ respectively. It confirmed that the BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL had prominent temperature elevation in response to NIR irradiation and possessed a remarkable potential as PTT agent.
After 8-h internalization, BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL was delivered in the lysosome of cells via FR-targeted effect (Figure S9-S11) and clathrin-dependent endocytosis pathway (Figure S12).[17] The low toxicity of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL to cells was verified with high viability of ${\sim}90\%$ by Cell Counting Kit-8 assays for both HeLa cells and normal HaCaT cells (Figure S13). The catalytic activity of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL for converting intracellular ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ was confirmed by a hypoxia indicator of $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{d p p})_{3}]\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ -treated hypoxic and normoxic HeLa cells (Figure S14 and S15). Furthermore, western blot and enzyme linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) analysis showed that the expression levels of hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF-1α) protein in BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMILtreated cells decreased to $34.9\%$ and $20.7\%$ in normoxia and hypoxia, respectively (Figure 3a and 3b), indicating that the hypoxia could be alleviated via $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ generation catalyzed by catalase in the outer layer of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL.
Upon a 660-nm laser irradiation, no obvious temperature change was observed (Figure S16), and the apoptosis of hypoxic cell was encouraged by inner BQ-MIL-mediated PDT (Figure S17). The apoptotic percentage of $52.1\%$ was 8.7-fold higher than that with BQ-MIL $@$ fMIL (Figure 3c), which signified that the extra oxygen supply could not only alleviate the hypoxia but also improve the PDT efficacy. Furthermore, the BQMIL $@$ cat-fMIL-mediated PTT induced a $28.7\%$ apoptotic ratio of hypoxic cells under 808 nm-laser irradiation. In comparison, a $75.6\%$ apoptosis ratio of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL-treated cells was achieved after 660-nm and 808-nm laser irradiation. Obviously, single PTT or PDT effect resulted in lower cancer therapy efficiency compared with that of combined PTT/PDT both in hypoxia and normoxia (Figure S18 and S19), suggesting that the BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL had a more preferable synergistic anticancer efficacy to induce cell apoptosis. More significantly, the fluorescence of Cy3-labeled substrate peptide increased with the deepened degree of cell apoptosis after different treatments (Figure 3d, S20 and S21), demonstrating the ability of BQMIL@cat-fMIL $^+$ PDT/PTT to induce cell apoptosis and activate caspase for therapeutic efficiency feedback (Figure S22).
Prior to assessing PDT effect of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL in vivo, the tumor HIF-1α staining assay was performed to track the change of tumor hypoxic conditions (Figure 4a). Compared with PBStreated and BQ-MIL $@$ fMIL-treated tumor slices with strong and bright immunofluorescence, those treated with BQ-MIL@catfMIL displayed lower expression, which could be regarded as marks of higher oxygen presence and relieved tumor hypoxia, thus breaking the limitation of PDT treatment. Then BQMIL $@$ cat-fMIL was injected intravenously into the tumor-bearing mice and accumulated in the tumor region efficiently compared to other major organs at 24 h post-injection (Figure 4b and S23). Afterwards, the mice treated with BQ-MIL@cat-fMIL were irradiated with $660~\mathsf{n m}$ laser $(150\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}}.$ for 10 min and the Cy3 fluorescence in tumors recovered more obviously at 24-h p.i. than that in the control group of BQ-MIL $@$ fMIL due to the sufficient oxygen supply for PDT (Figure 4c). Furthermore, the group treated by PDT $^+$ PTT displayed much stronger fluorescence than that with individual PDT and PTT, indicating the high synergistic therapy efficiency of BQ-MIL@cat-fMIL. The enhanced efficacy of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL was further evaluated by measuring the tumor volume for 14 days after treatments (Figure 4d). The mice treated with intravenous pure PBS or BQMIL@cat-fMIL without any irradiation displayed a rapid tumor growth, indicating that the laser exposure alone or BQ-MIL@catfMIL alone has no significant influence on the tumor growth. In contrast, BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL-mediated PDT could inhibit the growth of tumors to $28.2\%$ compared to $73.8\%$ in the BQMIL@fMIL-mediated PDT group, indicating that the substantial oxygen-supply enhanced $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation. Importantly, comparing with weaker antitumor effect of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL-mediated PTT, the tumor in the group BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL after the synergistic treatments of PTT/PDT was almost removed and decreased to the minimum of $7.3\%$ at 14 days. Therefore, BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL
