mediated PTT/PDT treatment had excellent therapeutic effect for in vivo solid tumor owing to the abundant $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ production and potent hyperthermia. Finally, the H&E staining analysis confirmed the low systemic toxicity of BQ-MIL $@$ cat-fMIL and the absence of pathological changes in the main organs as compared to healthy mice in the control (Figure S24).
In summary, a multifunctional homologous MOFs hybrid was designed with localized BQ-MIL inner and cat-MIL outer through stepwise in situ growth method. The tandem catalysis was realized using cat-MIL and BQ-MIL to catalyze the conversion of excess ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ to $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ , sequentially. The homologous MOF not only protected the catalytic activity of catalase under acidic tumor microenvironments to self-supply sufficient $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ but also greatly enhanced the $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ quantum yield of BQ. Since the extra oxygen supply could alleviate the hypoxia and improve the PDT efficacy, the apoptotic efficiency of MOF system was 8.7- fold higher than that without catalase. The PDT/PTT synergistic therapy further accelerated cell apoptosis and activated caspase-3 to cleave the $\mathtt{C y3}$ -pep for therapy efficiency feedback. Overall, the stepwise growth of MOF hybrid not only yielded two confined zones for precise assembly of individual components with enhanced activities, but also led to the rational tandem of their functionalities, providing an inspiring paradigm for developing a promising theranostics platform against hypoxic tumor microenvironment.
